## 📘 PART XIII: ADVANCED MOTOR CONTROL AND DRIVE SYSTEMS

### CHAPTER 42: MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION OF FIELD ORIENTED CONTROL (FOC)
To control a 3-phase BLDC or PMSM motor, we must generate a magnetic vector that is exactly 90 degrees ahead of the rotor's permanent magnets. This requires tracking the rotor angle ($\theta$) and applying complex math.

#### 42.1 The Clarke Transform
Converts 3-phase quantities ($a, b, c$) separated by 120 degrees into a 2-coordinate time-invariant orthogonal system ($\alpha, \beta$).
$$i_\alpha = \frac{2}{3} \left( i_a - \frac{1}{2}i_b - \frac{1}{2}i_c \right)$$
$$i_\beta = \frac{2}{3} \left( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i_b - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i_c \right)$$

#### 42.2 The Park Transform
Converts the stationary orthogonal system ($\alpha, \beta$) into a rotating reference frame ($d, q$) that rotates with the rotor.
$$i_d = i_\alpha \cos(\theta) + i_\beta \sin(\theta)$$
$$i_q = -i_\alpha \sin(\theta) + i_\beta \cos(\theta)$$
- **The Goal:** The PI controllers now regulate $i_d$ (flux producing, usually set to 0) and $i_q$ (torque producing). By converting AC signals to DC signals in the rotating frame, simple PI controllers can track them with zero steady-state error.

#### 42.3 Space Vector PWM (SVPWM)
Once the desired voltages ($V_d, V_q$) are calculated and transformed back to stationary frame ($V_\alpha, V_\beta$) via the Inverse Park Transform, SVPWM is used to determine the exact duty cycles for the 6 MOSFETs in the 3-phase inverter bridge to create the resultant voltage vector.

