This section provides rigorous definitions for the terminology used in the professional robotics industry.

- **ADC (Analog-to-Digital Converter):** A hardware peripheral that converts a continuous analog voltage into a discrete digital number.
- **Aliasing:** An effect that causes different signals to become indistinguishable when sampled too slowly.
- **Ampacity:** The maximum current, in amperes, that a conductor can carry continuously under the conditions of use without exceeding its temperature rating.
- **Back-EMF (Electromotive Force):** The voltage that opposes the change in current which induced it, generated by a spinning motor.
- **Bandwidth:** The range of frequencies within a given band, used for transmitting signals.
- **Baud Rate:** The rate at which information is transferred in a communication channel (bits per second).
- **BMS (Battery Management System):** An electronic system that manages a rechargeable battery (cell or battery pack).
- **Brownout:** A temporary drop in voltage that causes the microcontroller to reset.
- **C-Rate:** A measure of the rate at which a battery is discharged relative to its maximum capacity.
- **CAN Bus (Controller Area Network):** A robust vehicle bus standard designed to allow microcontrollers and devices to communicate with each other's applications without a host computer.
- **Closed-Loop Control:** A system that uses feedback to maintain a desired state.
- **Common Mode Rejection Ratio (CMRR):** The ability of a differential amplifier to reject signals common to both input leads.
- **Crosstalk:** Any phenomenon by which a signal transmitted on one circuit or channel of a transmission system creates an undesired effect in another circuit or channel.
- **Damping:** An influence within or upon an oscillatory system that has the effect of reducing, restricting or preventing its oscillations.
- **Dead-time:** A brief period in H-bridge switching where both transistors are off to prevent shoot-through.
- **DH Parameters (Denavit-Hartenberg):** Four parameters which are used to attach reference frames to the links of a robotic manipulator.
- **Differential Signaling:** A method for electrically transmitting information using two complementary signals.
- **Duty Cycle:** The ratio of 'Active' time to the total period of a PWM signal.
- **EMI (Electromagnetic Interference):** Disturbance that affects an electrical circuit due to either electromagnetic induction or electromagnetic radiation.
- **Encoder:** An electromechanical device that converts the angular position or motion of a shaft or axle to analog or digital signals.
- **ESR (Equivalent Series Resistance):** An ideal resistor in series with a capacitor representing its internal losses.
- **FMEA (Failure Modes and Effects Analysis):** A systematic, proactive method for evaluating a process to identify where and how it might fail.
- **FOC (Field Oriented Control):** A variable-frequency drive control method in which the stator currents of a three-phase AC electric motor are identified as two orthogonal components.
- **GaN (Gallium Nitride):** A wide-bandgap semiconductor material used in high-efficiency power electronics.
- **GPIO (General Purpose Input/Output):** An uncommitted digital signal pin on an integrated circuit.
- **Ground Loop:** An unwanted current path in a circuit resulting in noise and interference.
- **H-Bridge:** An electronic circuit that switches the polarity of a voltage applied to a load.
- **Hall Effect:** The production of a voltage difference across an electrical conductor which is transverse to an electric current in the conductor and to an applied magnetic field perpendicular to the current.
- **IMU (Inertial Measurement Unit):** An electronic device that measures and reports a body's specific force, angular rate, and sometimes the orientation of the body, using a combination of accelerometers, gyroscopes, and sometimes magnetometers.
- **Interrupt:** A signal to the processor emitted by hardware or software indicating an event that needs immediate attention.
- **Jacobian Matrix:** A matrix of all first-order partial derivatives of a vector-valued function.
- **Jitter:** The deviation from true periodicity of a presumably periodic signal.
- **Kalman Filter:** An algorithm that uses a series of measurements observed over time, containing statistical noise and other inaccuracies, and produces estimates of unknown variables that tend to be more accurate than those based on a single measurement alone.
- **Kinematics:** The branch of mechanics concerned with the motion of objects without reference to the forces which cause the motion.
- **LDO (Low Drop-Out):** A linear DC voltage regulator that can regulate the output voltage even when the supply voltage is very close to the output voltage.
- **LiDAR (Light Detection and Ranging):** A remote sensing method that uses light in the form of a pulsed laser to measure ranges (variable distances) to the Earth.
- **Logic Level Shifter:** A circuit used to translate signals from one logic level (e.g. 5V) to another (e.g. 3.3V).
- **MOSFET (Metal-Oxide-Semiconductor Field-Effect Transistor):** A type of field-effect transistor, fabricated by the controlled oxidation of silicon.
- **Nyquist Frequency:** Half of the sampling rate of a discrete signal processing system.
- **PID (Proportional-Integral-Derivative):** A control loop mechanism employing feedback that is widely used in industrial control systems and a variety of other applications requiring continuously modulated control.
- **PWM (Pulse Width Modulation):** A method of reducing the average power delivered by an electrical signal, by effectively chopping it up into discrete parts.
- **Quiescent Current:** The current drawn by a circuit when it is not performing any work or driving any load.
- **Reflow Soldering:** A process in which a solder paste is used to temporarily attach one or several electrical components to their contact pads, after which the entire assembly is subjected to controlled heat.
- **ROS (Robot Operating System):** A set of software libraries and tools that help you build robot applications.
- **Slew Rate:** The maximum rate of change of an amplifier's output voltage.
- **SMPS (Switch-Mode Power Supply):** An electronic power supply that incorporates a switching regulator to convert electrical power efficiently.
- **Stall Current:** The current drawn by a motor when the output rotational speed is zero.
- **Thermal Runaway:** A situation where an increase in temperature changes the conditions in a way that causes a further increase in temperature, often leading to a destructive result.
- **UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter):** A computer hardware device for asynchronous serial communication in which the data format and transmission speeds are configurable.
- **Voltage Sag:** A short duration reduction in voltage.
- **Watchdog Timer:** An electronic or software timer that is used to detect and recover from computer malfunctions.
- **Zener Diode:** A special type of diode designed to reliably allow current to flow backwards when a certain set reverse voltage, known as the Zener voltage, is reached.

